WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:09.768
Earlier in this lesson, one cook kept five pots going on one thread, in 358 milliseconds.

2
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:12.876
So here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

3
00:00:12.876 --> 00:00:15.282
A function's locals live with its thread.

4
00:00:15.282 --> 00:00:19.215
If the function pauses, and the thread walks off, where do they go?

5
00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:29.069
A running function keeps a frame on its thread's stack: its local variables, and the line it's up to.

6
00:00:29.069 --> 00:00:31.987
That's the cook's head: onions in, simmer next.

7
00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:35.983
Now she walks over to the salad, and her head fills up with lettuce.

8
00:00:35.983 --> 00:00:39.488
Ask her how much salt went into the soup, and she'll say: some.

9
00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:43.013
A thread is the same: the next code reuses that memory.

10
00:00:43.013 --> 00:00:45.964
So the place must be written down somewhere else.

11
00:00:46.967 --> 00:00:48.764
One fix is to never walk off.

12
00:00:48.764 --> 00:00:53.721
That's Thread.sleep, the cook standing at the pot, which is what we're avoiding.

13
00:00:53.933 --> 00:01:00.135
Another fix: copy the whole stack, like the order slip Java's virtual threads clip to the board.

14
00:01:00.135 --> 00:01:06.982
But that needs help from the JVM, the program that runs the code, and Kotlin coroutines don't ask for any.

15
00:01:06.982 --> 00:01:09.889
So the compiler writes the place down itself.

16
00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:12.239
The way a good cook does.

17
00:01:12.239 --> 00:01:19.751
She puts a bookmark at the next step, sticks a note on the page, onions: measured, sets the kitchen timer, and walks away.

18
00:01:19.751 --> 00:01:24.800
When it rings, she opens the book at the bookmark, reads the note, and carries on.

19
00:01:25.033 --> 00:01:25.868
Let's map it.

20
00:01:25.868 --> 00:01:27.345
The recipe is the code.

21
00:01:27.345 --> 00:01:29.720
The bookmark says which step is next.

22
00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:31.968
The note holds the local variables.

23
00:01:31.968 --> 00:01:35.435
And the cook is the thread, free while the book waits.

24
00:01:36.367 --> 00:01:38.434
Let's shrink it to one function.

25
00:01:38.434 --> 00:01:44.828
makeSauce waits for fryOnions, then for simmer: two calls that may pause, called suspension points.

26
00:01:45.033 --> 00:01:48.636
First, the compiler quietly changes the function.

27
00:01:48.636 --> 00:01:57.825
javap, a JDK tool for looking inside compiled classes, shows one more parameter, a Continuation, and a return type of Object.

28
00:01:58.033 --> 00:02:05.613
So it returns either the real sauce, or a marker, COROUTINE_SUSPENDED, which means: I've paused, I'll call you back.

29
00:02:05.833 --> 00:02:09.081
A three line function grew a parameter nobody wrote.

30
00:02:09.081 --> 00:02:11.892
If a teammate did that, you'd have questions.

31
00:02:11.892 --> 00:02:15.389
Here, that hidden parameter is how the bookmark travels.

32
00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:20.266
Next, the compiler cuts the body at each suspension point.

33
00:02:20.266 --> 00:02:21.466
makeSauce has two.

34
00:02:21.466 --> 00:02:23.332
How many steps will it make?

35
00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:32.162
Three: up to fryOnions, up to simmer, and after simmer, numbered by a label: 0, 1 and 2.

36
00:02:32.162 --> 00:02:35.892
Code with a counter like that is called a state machine.

37
00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:45.728
The bookmark is one small object the compiler writes: an int called label, for the page, and two fields, L$0 and L$1, for the sticky note.

38
00:02:45.933 --> 00:02:47.567
Let's follow one call.

39
00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:57.445
The label is 0, so step 0 writes the onions on the note, in L$0, sets the label to 1, and calls fryOnions, handing over the bookmark.

40
00:02:57.667 --> 00:03:04.332
fryOnions has to wait, so it returns COROUTINE_SUSPENDED, and makeSauce passes the marker straight up.

41
00:03:04.332 --> 00:03:10.736
Now there's no thread and no frame, just the bookmark, on the heap, the memory where objects live.

42
00:03:10.967 --> 00:03:16.800
When the onions are golden, something calls resumeWith on the bookmark, with the golden onions.

43
00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:20.729
makeSauce runs again, and a switch on the label jumps to step 1.

44
00:03:20.933 --> 00:03:27.535
Step 1 writes the golden onions in L$1, sets the label to 2, and calls simmer, which waits too.

45
00:03:27.535 --> 00:03:29.203
The thread leaves again.

46
00:03:29.433 --> 00:03:36.521
When simmer answers, resumeWith runs once more, and step 2 returns the sauce: sauce from golden onions.

47
00:03:36.521 --> 00:03:38.172
Nobody stood at the pan.

48
00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:39.907
makeSauce paused twice.

49
00:03:39.907 --> 00:03:42.595
So how many bookmark objects did it make?

50
00:03:45.567 --> 00:03:49.287
One, made when the call starts, and reused at every pause.

51
00:03:49.287 --> 00:03:51.532
Only the label and the note change.

52
00:03:52.433 --> 00:03:53.366
Now the names.

53
00:03:53.366 --> 00:03:58.431
The bookmark is a continuation: it knows how to carry on a paused coroutine.

54
00:03:58.431 --> 00:04:04.763
It has a context, the coroutine's settings, and resumeWith: the wait is over, here's the value.

55
00:04:04.967 --> 00:04:11.096
Passing what to do next as a hidden parameter is called continuation-passing style.

56
00:04:11.096 --> 00:04:13.385
And javap agrees: cases 0 to 2.

57
00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:19.510
Compare the slip on the board: the JVM copies every frame of a virtual thread.

58
00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:24.411
A coroutine keeps one small bookmark per suspend function still running.

59
00:04:24.633 --> 00:04:29.006
So a paused coroutine is cheap: you can have hundreds of thousands.

60
00:04:29.006 --> 00:04:34.097
And plain code can't call a suspend function: it has no bookmark to hand over.

61
00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:36.481
Here the picture breaks.

62
00:04:36.481 --> 00:04:40.038
A real cook hears the timer and walks back by herself.

63
00:04:40.038 --> 00:04:47.613
A paused coroutine does nothing until something calls resumeWith: a library timer, or the code that got the answer.

64
00:04:47.833 --> 00:04:51.309
Forget to call it, and it waits forever, very politely.

65
00:04:51.309 --> 00:04:53.457
So somebody has to ring the timer.

66
00:04:54.267 --> 00:04:56.985
Here's the Kotlin, with Java beside it.

67
00:04:56.985 --> 00:04:59.495
Kotlin's compiler writes a bookmark.

68
00:04:59.495 --> 00:05:03.887
Java's virtual thread blocks, and the JVM saves the whole slip.

69
00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:07.970
So, where do the locals go when the thread walks off?

70
00:05:07.970 --> 00:05:10.068
Onto a sticky note, in a bookmark.

71
00:05:10.068 --> 00:05:16.176
A suspended coroutine is a bookmark, not a thread, and something has to call resumeWith to wake it.

72
00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:17.262
Quick check.

73
00:05:17.262 --> 00:05:19.490
fryOnions waits once, on delay.

74
00:05:19.490 --> 00:05:22.436
How many steps does the compiler give it?

75
00:05:25.433 --> 00:05:25.812
Two.

76
00:05:25.812 --> 00:05:30.639
Label 0 runs up to the delay, and label 1 after it.

77
00:05:30.639 --> 00:05:33.005
javap shows cases 0 to 1.
